        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Previous experience has shown that space flight induces loss of bone mass, which continues throughout extended-duration missions. Mineral is shed from bones during flight as a result of skeletal unloading, thereby increasing calcium and sodium excretion in the urine. Loss of bone tissue is most profound in weight-bearing bones such as the <I>os calcis</I>. Understanding the mechanisms underlying this loss and the recovery process is critical for protecting crew safety and health both during flight and after return to Earth. Loss of bone and muscle also increase the solute load in urine, which in turn increases the risk of renal-stone formation. These studies will test the hypothesis that space flight alters calcium homeostasis, calcium kinetics and bone mineral metabolism. <P>

Specific Aims:<br>
1. Determine the effects of space flight on calcium absorption, calcium kinetics, and bone calcium.<br>
2. Assess the impact of space flight on biochemical markers of bone metabolism and bone/calcium regulating hormones.<br>
3. Measure bone-related biochemical and hormonal changes during the recovery period.<br> 
<p>
The implementation of this experiment on Shuttle flights will allow for an understanding
of the initial changes in bone and calcium metabolism during the initial adaptation to space flight. This information, in conjunction with implementation on long-term missions (i.e., ISS) will allow for a full understanding of weightlessness-induced bone loss, and will enable countermeasure development.<br>
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Calcium kinetics and biochemical markers of bone metabolism were anticipated to be measured before, during and after flight. Additional data was expected via DEXA scans.
<P>
Inflight, markers of calcium homeostasis and bone mineral metabolism was measured in serum and urine. Calcium kinetics (absorption, bone formation, and bone resorption) was assessed using a dual-isotope tracer protocol, which involves one or more sample collections on specified days. Testing included the collection of blood, urine, feces, and saliva as well as an administration of oral and intravenous doses of calcium. Depending on the session, <sup>46</sup>Ca or <sup>48</sup>Ca was given orally followed by a carrier dose of natural calcium (CaCO<sub>3</sub> capsules); <sup>42</sup>Ca was injected intravenously an hour after ingestion of the calcium tracer. Dietary intake was assessed using a barcode reader.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
Results from this experiment aren’t available.
<P>
        </div>

